Day 2 – SQL Intermediate

📅 Date: September 5, 2026

🎯 Objective

Continued my SQL learning journey by moving from basic queries to intermediate SQL concepts and practical query solving.

The focus was on filtering, grouping, JOINs, NULL handling, subqueries and conditional logic.

📚 Topics Covered

AND

OR

IN

BETWEEN

LIKE

Wildcards (% and _)

IS NULL

IS NOT NULL

GROUP BY

Aggregate Functions

HAVING

INNER JOIN

LEFT JOIN

Finding unmatched records

Subqueries

CASE

Second Highest Salary

💻 Practice Questions

Filtering

Find employees based on multiple conditions using AND.

Find employees satisfying at least one condition using OR.

Find employees belonging to specific departments using IN.

Find employees whose salary falls within a specific range using BETWEEN.

Find employees whose names start with a particular letter using LIKE.

Find names having an exact number of characters using _.

Find names containing a particular character using %.

NULL Handling

Find employees whose department ID is NULL.

Find employees whose department ID is NOT NULL.

GROUP BY & HAVING

Find the number of employees in each department.

Find the average salary in each department.

Find departments having more than a specific number of employees.

Find departments where the average salary is greater than a specified amount.

JOINs

Display employee details along with department names using INNER JOIN.

Display all employees along with their department names using LEFT JOIN.

Find employees who do not have a matching department.

Subqueries

Find employees earning more than the company average salary.

Find the employee(s) earning the maximum salary.

Find the second-highest salary.

CASE

Categorize employees into High, Medium and Low salary groups using CASE.

🧠 Key Learnings

AND / OR

Learned how to combine multiple conditions when filtering records.

IN

Learned how to check whether a value belongs to a list of possible values.

BETWEEN

Learned how to filter values within a specific range.

LIKE

Learned pattern matching using % and _.

-- Starts with A

WHERE employee_name LIKE 'A%';

-- Starts with A and contains exactly 4 characters

WHERE employee_name LIKE 'A___';

% represents any number of characters, while _ represents exactly one character.

NULL Handling

Learned that NULL values should be checked using:

IS NULL

IS NOT NULL

GROUP BY & HAVING

Improved my understanding of grouping records and filtering grouped results.

JOINs

Practiced combining information from multiple tables using:

INNER JOIN

LEFT JOIN

Subqueries

Learned how one SQL query can be used inside another query to solve problems such as finding employees earning above the average salary.

CASE

Practiced using CASE statements to apply conditional logic and categorize data.

💼 ETL / Data Engineering Relevance

The concepts learned today are useful in ETL and Data Engineering for:

Filtering source data.

Applying business rules.

Joining multiple source tables.

Identifying missing records.

Performing data validation.

Aggregating data.

Creating transformation logic.

Identifying data quality issues.

